WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.293
llama.cpp, compiled to WebAssembly, running in a browser tab.

00:00:04.293 --> 00:00:08.563
Version four claims up to 2.8 times faster on Chrome.

00:00:08.943 --> 00:00:13.256
And no size limit, in a runtime where every instance stops at four gibibytes.

00:00:16.002 --> 00:00:16.701
I'm Ringo.

00:00:16.701 --> 00:00:20.898
Two changes explain this release: the kernels the build finally compiles, and

00:00:20.898 --> 00:00:22.997
the workers that carry the layers.

00:00:24.683 --> 00:00:28.886
Emscripten's CMake toolchain tells the build it is targeting x86.

00:00:29.286 --> 00:00:32.202
So ggml never compiled its wasm kernels.

00:00:32.202 --> 00:00:34.702
Every quantized matmul ran generic C.

00:00:35.102 --> 00:00:36.609
One flag fixes the detection.

00:00:36.609 --> 00:00:39.320
On its own it was worth about twenty percent.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:43.769
The jump came from relaxed SIMD dot products on top: four 8-bit

00:00:43.769 --> 00:00:47.818
dot products where the old path widened every weight to sixteen bits.

00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:53.104
Back on 32-bit memory, one instance holds four gibibytes.

00:00:53.104 --> 00:00:55.686
A bigger model simply does not fit.

00:00:56.106 --> 00:00:59.060
So the load measures the CPU side first.

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:04.229
Over 3584 mebibytes, it asks for one worker per three gibibytes and starts again.

00:01:04.649 --> 00:01:07.895
With a GPU, its layers are placed first, exactly as many

00:01:07.895 --> 00:01:09.075
as you asked for.

00:01:09.495 --> 00:01:13.481
llama.cpp's own fit then fills each worker until one gibibyte of

00:01:13.481 --> 00:01:14.930
its four is left.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:18.534
The rest stays in the main instance, with the token embeddings.

00:01:18.534 --> 00:01:20.271
A worker can end up empty.

00:01:21.808 --> 00:01:26.144
llama.cpp thinks it is talking to another machine over ggml-rpc.

00:01:26.144 --> 00:01:28.745
The socket is a shared buffer.

00:01:29.145 --> 00:01:31.706
Two rings per worker, one each way.

00:01:31.706 --> 00:01:35.365
One copy in, one copy out, and llama.cpp is unchanged.

00:01:37.367 --> 00:01:40.962
The author's numbers, Chrome on an M5 Max, CPU only.

00:01:41.342 --> 00:01:49.485
The headline 2.8 is prefill on LFM2.5 1.2B: 73 to 207 tokens a second.

00:01:49.865 --> 00:01:53.945
And the author says plainly that the two changes were never measured apart.

00:01:55.092 --> 00:02:01.880
On an older Intel desktop, in headless Chrome, the doubling holds: 9.10 to 20.80.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:07.698
Native llama.cpp on the same cores did 213.71, roughly ten times more.

00:02:09.383 --> 00:02:11.843
Hybrid gets a model running, not running fast.

00:02:11.843 --> 00:02:14.919
Twenty layers on the GPU: two and a half times.

00:02:14.919 --> 00:02:16.149
All of them: twenty.

00:02:17.267 --> 00:02:19.774
Put the whole model on WebGPU when it fits.

00:02:19.774 --> 00:02:23.116
The workers and the hybrid split are for when it does not.

00:02:25.192 --> 00:02:29.950
llama.cpp in a tab: faster because it compiles its own kernels, bigger

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:31.932
because it borrows more instances.

00:02:32.352 --> 00:02:34.435
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:34.435 --> 00:02:35.030
I'm Ringo.

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:35.328
Bye!
